{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LkiJioUfXtWb5SWjb5q5hK","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent! Now I have enough information to provide a comprehensive report. Let me verify there's no cmd/kill and summarize my findings:\n\n## Summary of Findings\n\n### 1. Flag Parsing Pattern\n\nThe repository uses **manual flag parsing** (no external library like `flag` or `pflag`). The pattern is consistent across utilities:\n\n**Key characteristics:**\n- Manual string parsing with loops through `os.Args[1:]`\n- Recognition of three flag types:\n  - `--` terminates flag processing\n  - Long flags: `--flag` and `--flag=value`\n  - Short flags: `-f` and combined `-fvn`\n- Separate functions: `parseArgs()` (main dispatcher), `parseLongFlag()` (long options), `parseShortFlags()` (short options)\n- Return values typically indicate \"how many args were consumed\" to advance the loop pointer\n\n**Examples:**\n- **cmd/sort/main.go** (lines 124-156): Complex pattern with `parseLongPrefix()` for `--flag=value` variants, `parseLongFlag()` for dispatch, and `parseShortFlags()` for combined flags. Returns consumed count.\n- **cmd/head/main.go** (lines 111-143): Similar structure, handles both `--lines=10` and `--lines 10` syntax.\n- **cmd/tr/main.go** (lines 39-91): Simpler approach with inline flag handling and operand collection.\n- **cmd/cat/main.go** (lines 55-104): Minimal approach using global flag variables and simple loop.\n\nThe pattern emphasizes:\n- No external dependencies (pure Go stdlib)\n- Detailed error messages with context\n- Support for both GNU long and short flags\n- Proper handling of argument terminators (`--`)\n\n### 2. DiffTest Struct Fields\n\nLocated in `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5424/pkg/testutils/difftest.go` (lines 46-55):\n\n```go\ntype DiffTest struct {\n    Name          string\n    Args          []string\n    Stdin         []byte\n    Env           []string\n    WorkDir       string\n    ExitCode      int\n    Normalize     []NormalizeFunc\n    ExpectedFiles map[string][]byte\n}\n```\n\n**Field details:**\n- `Name`: Identifies the subtest (used with `t.Run`)\n- `Args`: Command-line arguments passed to both binaries\n- `Stdin`: Raw stdin bytes (`nil` = EOF immediately; empty slice `[]byte{}` = open stdin immediately closed)\n- `Env`: KEY=VALUE pairs merged into inherited environment (LC_ALL=C applied by default as override)\n- `WorkDir`: Working directory (defaults to `t.TempDir()` if empty)\n- `ExitCode`: Expected exit code (defaults to 0)\n- `Normalize`: Slice of `NormalizeFunc` applied in order to both stdout and stderr before comparison\n- `ExpectedFiles`: Map of relative file paths to expected contents for post-execution validation\n\nAdditional feature: `TimestampNormalizer` is a built-in normalizer for common timestamp patterns.\n\n### 3. cmd/kill Status\n\n**Does NOT exist.** The cmd/ directory contains 42 utilities but `kill` is not among them. The available signal-handling utilities in the repository do not include a `kill` command implementation.\n\nThe closest signal-related code in the repository would be in the `timeout` command (which handles signals) or the general signal handling in `pkg/sys`, but there's no dedicated signal parsing module like would be found in a `kill` implementation."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0257407e-6ee4-42e2-a7f2-a490be4c0a8c","uuid":"91d1c1c1-d305-4e91-9f53-9664c1c0ad2e","timestamp":"2026-05-21T15:08:10.558Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In the repo at /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5424, I need to understand:\n\n1. How do other cmd/ utilities parse GNU-style flags? Look at a few examples (e.g., cmd/sort/main.go, cmd/head/main.go, cmd/kill/main.go) to see the pattern used. Do they use a library or manual parsing?\n\n2. What fields does the testutils.DiffTest struct hav