{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01356WHLSFVxGzQ6joUUKEUj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting. So:\n- SIGTERM: gtimeout exits normally with code 124 (not killed by signal)\n- SIGKILL: gtimeout is killed by SIGKILL (exits via signal, ExitCode=-1)\n- SIGHUP: gtimeout exits normally with code 124\n\nSo for SIGKILL specifically, gtimeout dies because it sends SIGKILL to its own process group (which includes itself). For SIGTERM and SIGHUP, it catches the signal and handles it.\n\nFor our implementation to match:\n- SIGTERM/HUP/etc: we already exit 124 correctly\n\n\n- SIGKILL: we need to also die by SIGKILL\n\nThe key insight is that GNU timeout creates its own process group and puts the child in it. When it sends SIGKILL to the group, both processes die. For catchable signals, it intercepts them first, waits for the child, then exits with 124. To match this behavior, I need to send SIGKILL to ourselves after sending it to the child group, but only when the signal is actually SIGKILL and we're not in foreground mode.\n\nThe tricky part is that if we kill ourselves from the timer callback (running in a goroutine), we die immediately without waiting for `cmd.Wait()` to complete in the main goroutine. But that's actually the right behavior—it matches what gtimeout does. I should verify whether gtimeout with `--preserve-status -s KILL` also dies by SIGKILL itself.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2903,"cache_read_input_tokens":37510,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2903},"output_tokens":30,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0257407e-6ee4-42e2-a7f2-a490be4c0a8c","uuid":"a59073df-b927-46cb-b093-bf17e97a1bde"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01356WHLSFVxGzQ6joUUKEUj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015HNPiL5fVCMzpgRWeT6LQM","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat > /tmp/test_kill2.go << 'GOEOF'\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"syscall\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n\t// preserve-status + KILL\n\tcmd := exec.Command(\"gtimeout\", \"--preserve-status\", \"-s\", \"KILL\", \"0.01\", \"sleep\", \"10\")\n\terr := cmd.Run()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tif exitErr, ok := err.(*exec.ExitError); ok {\n\t\t\tstatus := exitErr.Sys().(syscall.WaitStatus)\n\t\t\tfmt.Printf(\"preserve+KILL: ExitCode=%d Signaled=%v\", exitErr.ExitCode(), status.Signaled())\n\t\t\tif status.Signaled() {\n\t\t\t\tfmt.Printf(\" Signal=%d\", status.Signal())\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfmt.Println()\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// kill-after with SIGKILL escalation \n\tcmd2 := exec.Command(\"gtimeout\", \"-k\", \"0.01\", \"0.01\", \"sleep\", \"10\")\n\terr2 := cmd2.Run()\n\tif err2 != nil {\n\t\tif exitErr, ok := err2.(*exec.ExitError); ok {\n\t\t\tstatus := exitErr.Sys().(syscall.WaitStatus)\n\t\t\tfmt.Printf(\"kill-after: ExitCode=%d Signaled=%v\", exitErr.ExitCode(), status.Signaled())\n\t\t\tif status.Signaled() {\n\t\t\t\tfmt.Printf(\" Signal=%d\", status.Signal())\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif status.Exited() {\n\t\t\t\tfmt.Printf(\" ExitStatus=%d\", status.ExitStatus())\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfmt.Println()\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\nGOEOF\ngo run /tmp/test_kill2.go","description":"Test gtimeout kill-after and preserve-status+KILL"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2903,"cache_read_input_tokens":37510,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2903},"output_tokens":30,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0257407e-6ee4-4