even more carefully. When GNU timeout has `-s KILL`:\n1. Timer fires, sends SIGKILL to process group 0 (its own group)\n2. Both GNU timeout and child die by SIGKILL\n3. Parent sees GNU timeout died by signal 9\n\nWhen GNU timeout has `-k 0.01`:\n1. Timer fires, sends SIGTERM to process group 0\n2. GNU timeout catches SIGTERM (doesn't die)\n3. Kill-after timer fires, sends SIGKILL to... the child specifically? Or to process group 0?\n\nIf kill-after also sends to process group 0 with SIGKILL, GNU timeout would die. But we observed it exits 124. So kill-after must send SIGKILL differently, probably just to the child.\n\nLooking at the GNU coreutils source, the kill-after signal is sent specifically to the monitored process/group, not to the entire process group 0. Or maybe the monitoring_pid has changed...\n\nYou know what, I'll just handle it pragmatically: for initial timeout with SIGKILL I'll kill ourselves to match GNU behavior, but for kill-after escalation I'll target only the child process so we can report exit 124. Writing the signal handler...\n\nWhen SIGKILL hits our process, everything stops immediately—the timer goroutine, main goroutine, all of it. The parent sees us killed by signal 9, which matches gtimeout's behavior. For the kill-after case, we just send SIGKILL to the child without killing ourselves. There's an edge case where sendSignal might fail, but that's fine since both SIGKILL calls are best-effort anyway. Now I need to verify the test expectations align with how the framework reports exit codes for signal deaths. exit:    124\n```\n\nThe framework uses -1 for signal deaths. So I need to set ExitCode to... hmm, but the DiffTest struct has ExitCode int. Can I set it to -1?\n\nActually wait, looking at the output more carefully: `ref exit: -1` and `go exit: 124`. The test said `ExitCode: 137`. The framework is comparing ref (-1) against go (124) and finding a mismatch. So the test framework comparison uses the raw exit code from exec.ExitError.ExitCode(), which returns -1 for signal deaths.\n\nSo if I fix our binary to also die by SIGKILL (matching ref at -1), both would be -1 and I should set ExitCode: -1 in the test... but is that how the test framework works? Let me check.\n\nLooking at the diff test output format, it says:\n```\nref exit:    -1\ngo  exit:    124\n```\n\nIf both are -1, they'd match and pass. The ExitCode field in DiffTest is probably just for documentation or an additional assertion—what matters is that the ref and go behaviors align. If I make our binary exit with SIGKILL like the reference does, both will return -1 and the test should pass.\n\nSo the fix is: when the initial timeout signal is SIGKILL, send SIGKILL directly instead of escalating. That way both binaries die the same way.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1031,"cache_read_input_tokens":40413,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1031},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0257407e-6ee4-42e2-a7f2-a490be4c0a8c","uuid":"711108c2-132c-42e7-bc4a-5bbbb04dda6c"}
